WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.904
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.635 --> 00:00:09.260
JOHN GABRIELI: Our
laboratory is really

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.990
interested in understanding
how children's minds

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.850
and brains grow and develop.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.710
The field has largely used this
term, the 30 million word gap.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.450
And that comes out of research,
where researchers recorded

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.330
home conversations between
parents and children

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.090
of various levels of family
income and education.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.770
And they estimated,
or they calculated,

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.500
that a child who arrives at
the beginning of school, who

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.560
comes from high income,
high education family

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.530
has heard 30 million
more words spoken to him

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.890
or her than a child who comes
from a family where there's

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.750
lesser education
and lesser income.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.670
RACHEL ROMEO: So the question is
why and what do we do about it.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.050
We know that the
language exposure

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.440
is contributing to the
children's language abilities.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.540
But what is the
mechanism underlying

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.610
this input-output relationship?

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.340
How is the language
exposure translating

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.002
into a language gap?

00:01:00.002 --> 00:01:01.460
JOHN GABRIELI: And
the second thing

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.810
we were very interested in is,
although we know that this word

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.130
gap is associated
with family income,

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.910
how close is that relationship.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.840
So we had to go look for
lots of parents with kids

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.000
from a broad range and
diverse backgrounds,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.340
in terms of income
and education at home,

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.570
and ask them to wear
a device at home

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.690
so we could record
the number of words

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.880
that were spoken between
parents and children,

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.190
as well as a number of
conversational turns.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:29.570
RACHEL ROMEO: We had a small
fleet of audio recorders

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.660
that children would actually
wear in a shirt pocket.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.210
And they would wear
these from the time

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.610
they woke up to the time
they went to bed at night.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.390
And then we would use speech
recognition algorithms

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.390
to determine how much
speech they had been hearing

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.030
and how much speech they had
been producing themselves,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.310
and then how many
back and forth turns

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.705
they'd been taking with other
conversational partners.

00:01:51.705 --> 00:01:53.580
JOHN GABRIELI: And then
the children came in,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.460
and this was the exotic
part of the study.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.497
They went into an MRI scanner.

00:01:57.497 --> 00:02:00.080
And we recorded their brains as
they heard little stories that

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.120
were spoken to them
while their brains were

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.730
being monitored through
functional magnetic resonance

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:05.450
imaging.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.790
RACHEL ROMEO: Neuroimaging
was pretty labor intensive,

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:08.509
as well.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:11.870
Working with children
as young as four,

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.690
it's very difficult to
do MRI scans with them.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.480
Because they have to lie
perfectly still so that we

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.920
can get nice, clear images.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.790
So I'm sitting next
to our mock scanner.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.950
It's a wooden replica of
what the MRI scanner actually

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:28.850
looks like.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.660
And if they move too much,
the motion sensitive camera

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.400
will cut off their video.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.870
So it's kind of a conditioned
training for these children

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:42.320
to teach themselves to lie still
while they're doing the imaging

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:43.160
sessions.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.530
After we finished
collecting all of the data,

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.710
we were excited to look at how
the home language experience

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:50.970
related to the brain functions.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.210
And what we were
surprised to find

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.190
is that the number
of adult words

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.970
didn't seem to matter at
all for the brain function.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.530
What mattered was the number
of conversational turns.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:05.030
JOHN GABRIELI: The part of the
brain that showed the biggest

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.120
difference, in relation to the
conversational turns at home

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.060
is one of the two major
language areas of the brain that

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.710
is called Broca's area.

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:16.310
And what we saw was that
the more these children had

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.760
conversational turns at
home, the more this area

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.800
was engaged as they heard
stories spoken to them while we

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:22.897
were imaging their brains.

00:03:22.897 --> 00:03:24.980
The second thing we were
surprised by a little bit

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:28.130
was that it didn't matter
the education or income

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:29.150
level of the family.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.130
What mattered was how
often these conversations

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:32.510
turned back and forth.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.310
RACHEL ROMEO: So we're
taking these results

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.670
as a social optimism of sorts.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.000
Because socioeconomic
status, or SES,

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.150
is largely difficult to change.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.340
But a conversation
with your child

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:49.610
is something much more
simple, much more malleable.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:51.380
JOHN GABRIELI: So
our next goal is

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.417
to work on
interventions that will

00:03:53.417 --> 00:03:56.000
help families have more of these
conversations back and forth.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.010
Can we not only describe
this relationship,

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.220
but can we foster and enhance it
so more parents are comfortable

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.810
and supported in
having these kinds

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.018
of critical conversational
turns with their children.

00:04:05.018 --> 00:04:07.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

